\title{How-possibly and how-actually:\\ what a resting-state manifold explains}
\author{P.~Mahon \\ \small Skeleton draft v2 (backward-error framing); every sentence is a draft for rewrite. Conditional on the numerical gates.}
\date{17 November 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section*{Opening}
\textcite{fousek2024} offer a mechanistic account of the brain's resting state: a network of bistable regions, coupled through a measured connectome and driven by noise, settles onto a low-dimensional manifold whose structured flow produces the dynamic functional connectivity, the co-activation events and the cascades seen in resting fMRI and EEG. I will not dispute the model's internal analysis, which I think is sound. I will ask what kind of explanation the paper gives, and whether its evidence supports that kind. I will argue that the paper supplies a \emph{how-possibly} explanation of its phenomena, in \textcite{craver2006}'s sense of a model that shows how a mechanism could produce them, and presents it as a \emph{how-actually} explanation, one that shows how the mechanism does; that the strongest reply available to the authors relocates this problem rather than removing it; and that, stated as what it is, the paper is the first half of a well-formed explanatory project whose second half is testable and untested. A how-possibly model is a precondition of a how-actually one, not a failed version of it: a mechanism whose output is nowhere near the data is not a candidate at all. The question is what, beyond nearness, is needed, and in one line my answer will be that the paper establishes a backward error, the data are the exact output of a nearby model, in a seminorm with a large kernel, and reports it as a forward error, a bound on which models the data admit.

I will refer to the resting-state features the paper sets out to explain as `the phenomena', in the sense of \textcite{bogen1988}, to the network model as `the mechanism', and to the low-dimensional object the model produces as `the manifold'. First I will say what the phenomena are and why the choice of them matters. Following this I will set out the standard of explanation the paper adopts, in its own words, and the objection that standard invites. I will then put the best reply I can construct on the authors' behalf, assess it, and end with what I take to be the two positions that remain consistent.

\section{The explanandum: data and phenomena}
Definitions first. By `data' I mean the recorded BOLD and EEG time series. By `phenomena' I mean, following \textcite{bogen1988}, the stable patterns extracted from data by a procedure: here, the fluidity of sliding-window functional connectivity (the variance of the correlations between windowed connectivity matrices), the share of variance carried by two principal components, and the statistics of high-amplitude co-activation events. A phenomenon is only as fine as the statistic that defines it.

\EX{a homely case of data versus phenomenon: the readings of a bathroom scale versus ``I have lost weight this month''; the second is what a diet explains, and it is only as fine as the averaging that produced it.}

Three facts about these particular phenomena will matter. The variance share of two components is a function of the sample covariance alone. The histogram statistic used to match model to data depends only on the multiset of correlation values, not on their order in time. The fluidity index is nearly order-free as well, and is positive for any stationary signal, because windowed correlations are noisy estimates with correlated errors. \NUM{the exact invariance statements, from the note's lemmas} None of the three can tell switching dynamics from a static covariance with autocorrelation. This is not yet an objection; it is a description of what was chosen to be explained.

\section{The explanatory claim and its standard}
The paper is explicit about what it wants. Earlier models, it says, ``leave a gap in the description of a mechanism''; to explain ``is not merely to redescribe one regularity \ldots\ as a series of several'', but to reveal ``the productive relation between causal activities linked to their constituent entities'' \parencite[Introduction]{fousek2024}. This is mechanistic explanation in the sense of \textcite{mdc2000} and \textcite{craver2007}: entities (regions, tracts, bistable populations) and activities (noise-driven switching between up and down states), organized so as to produce the phenomenon.

The standard carries its own tests, and I will hold the paper to them because the paper chose them. First, \textcite{craver2006} distinguishes a \emph{how-possibly} model, which shows that a mechanism could produce the phenomenon, from a \emph{how-actually} model, which shows that it does; only the second explains, although the first is required for it, since the space of candidate mechanisms has to be charted before one can be singled out. Second, the components of a how-actually model must be \emph{constitutively relevant}, which Craver cashes out as mutual manipulability: intervening on a part changes the whole, and intervening on the whole changes the part \parencite{craver2007}. The paper disclaims curve-fitting (``our goal here is not to generate in silico observables that are as close as possible to the empirical one''). It does not disclaim realism. It asserts a mechanism of the brain.

\section{The objection: underdetermination of the mechanism by the phenomena}
Here is the objection in its plainest form. The phenomena of \S1 are coarse functionals of the data. Many generative hypotheses entail the same values of those functionals, including hypotheses with no mechanistic content at all: a stationary Gaussian process with the right covariance and spectrum reproduces windowed dFC statistics \parencite{hindriks2016,liegeois2017,laumann2017} and co-activation events \parencite{novelli2022}; simple linear models on the same connectome predict functional connectivity as well as nonlinear network models \parencite{messe2014,messe2015}; and in mechanistic models generally, most parameter directions barely move the predictions \parencite{gutenkunst2007,machta2013,transtrum2015}, so that disparate parameter sets yield the same activity \parencite{prinz2004}. \NUM{in the reimplementation, the fraction of the coupling and noise grid, and of rewired connectomes, that lands within tolerance of the working point}

I want to be precise about which underdetermination this is. It is not the Duhem--Quine kind, where any hypothesis can be saved by adjusting auxiliaries. It is \emph{contrastive}, in the sense \textcite{laudan1991} separate from holism: the evidence fails to discriminate the proposed mechanism from a wide class of alternatives that entail the same phenomena. The inference from ``the model produces the phenomena'' to ``the model's mechanism is the brain's'' is then an inference to \emph{an} explanation, not to the best \parencite{lipton2004}. In Craver's terms, a how-possibly model has been offered as how-actually. The sloppy-model and surrogate results are the empirical measure of how wide the contrast class is. I will refer to this as `the dart-board objection': throw a dart at the space of models and you hit one that reproduces the phenomena.

\EX{the dart board itself, or a lock that many keys open.}

The same point can be put in the language of numerical analysis, which I find clearer. Write $\Psi$ for the map from the model's parameters to the law of the data it generates, and $\varphi$ for the statistic that defines a phenomenon. \textcite{wilkinson1963}'s backward-error question is whether the computed answer is the exact answer to a nearby problem; here, whether the data are the exact output of a nearby model, measured in the seminorm $\varphi$ induces. The paper establishes that this backward error is small. What how-actually requires is a bound on the \emph{forward} error, the size of the set of parameters compatible with the data in the coordinates that distinguish mechanisms, and forward error is backward error times a condition number \parencite{higham2002}. For these models that condition number spans many decades along most parameter directions \parencite{transtrum2015}, and along the directions the statistic cannot see at all, those in its kernel, it is infinite: the compatible set is unbounded there however small the backward error. The paper computes a backward error in a seminorm with a large kernel and reports it as a forward error.

\section{On behalf of the authors: constitutive warrant}
On behalf of the authors one could reply as follows, and I think this is the strongest reply available. The phenomena were never the warrant for the mechanism; they are the explanandum. The warrant comes from the independent evidence for the components, in the manner of \textcite{glymour1980}'s bootstrapping, or, in the numerical idiom, a \emph{structured} backward error in which the measured components are pinned and only the unmeasured ones may vary: the connectome is measured, not fitted; the node model is derived exactly from spiking neurons \parencite{montbrio2015}; bistable regional activity is reported in data \parencite[refs 39--41 therein]{fousek2024}. The feature match establishes sufficiency, which is all it was asked to establish. Constitutive relevance is shown by manipulation: sweep the coupling and the phenomena come and go; replace the connectome with an all-to-all network and they vanish.

Moreover, the reply continues, the contrast class of \S3 is mis-specified. A stationary Gaussian process is a \emph{phenomenal} model in Craver's own sense, his Ptolemaic case: descriptively adequate and explanatorily empty, because it posits no components \parencite{craver2006}. It cannot compete with a mechanism, so it cannot underdetermine one. The relevant contrast class is mechanisms with independently warranted parts, which is far smaller than ``all generators''. And the manifold is not a fitted object but a representational bridge between the parts and the phenomena. It earns its keep by yielding predictions outside the phenomena used for selection: the up-state frequency should rise with a region's in-strength; the time spent in cascades should fall along the cortical hierarchy; each subject's connectome should fix that subject's working point. On Lakatos's criterion, novel prediction is the mark of a progressive programme \parencite{lakatos1970}.

I think this reply is right about two things. A phenomenal surrogate cannot refute a mechanism. And the dart board must be thrown over mechanisms, not over descriptions.

\section{Rejoinder: the contrast class is still populated, and the warrant is uneven}
Granting the phenomenal/mechanistic distinction relocates the underdetermination without dissolving it, for four reasons.

First, the class of mechanisms with warranted parts that entail these phenomena is still large. Regions near a Hopf bifurcation, excitable regions, and metastable regimes on the same connectome all reproduce dynamic functional connectivity \parencite{hansen2015,deco2017,messe2015}. These are conceived alternatives, not Stanford's unconceived ones \parencite{stanford2006}, so the burden of discriminating among them is live now. The phenomena of \S1 cannot bear it.

Second, bootstrapping requires that each component be warranted \emph{in the regime the mechanism invokes}. The connectome is. The bistable regime is a parameter choice the authors themselves describe as characteristic of sleep. Regional homogeneity is known to be false. The noise is unmeasured. The component with the least independent warrant is exactly the activity the mechanism needs: noise-driven switching between up and down states in the awake resting brain.

Third, the manipulations were performed on the model. On the interventionist account \parencite{woodward2003}, that establishes constitutive relevance within the model; mutual manipulability of the brain would require interventions on the brain, or natural experiments that stand in for them.

Fourth, the novel predictions, which would settle the matter, are checked inside the model (the frequency law) or reported without a test (the hierarchy trend, whose spatial match the authors say was not recovered), and the per-subject result correlates two quantities that peak together by construction. \NUM{the within-row rewiring gate: whether the connectome's topology, beyond its in-strength distribution, does any work at all}

The explanation is therefore constitutive in form and how-possibly in evidential status.

\section{Two positions that remain}
Two positions survive, and they are not the same paper.

The constructive-empiricist position \parencite{vanfraassen1980}: the model is empirically adequate with respect to the chosen observables, and the manifold is a device for thinking about the model. This is consistent, but it has a cost. Adequacy is relative to observables, and these observables cannot register the device's distinctive content, so adequacy is cheap, and ``the brain's resting state manifold'' becomes a manner of speaking. On this reading the paper's title claims more than its position allows.

The realist position, which the authors take: the manifold and the switching mechanism are in the brain. Then what is owed is the how-actually programme that the reply of \S4 itself describes, and in the terms of \S3 there are exactly four ways to shrink the set of models the data admit, which are not the same move: refine the statistic until its kernel is small (a finer norm); pin more components by independent measurement in the regime invoked (a smaller parameter space); add equations the fibre must also satisfy (interventions and cross-modal constraints); and weight the fibre by a stated reference measure (a posterior, which does not shrink the set but says how much of it one believes). Concretely: warrant the activity independently, or restrict the scope to states where up/down bistability is established and test whether the cascade and connectivity signatures track it across sleep stages. Test the derived predictions outside the model: up-state frequency against in-strength in source-localized MEG; per-subject working points out of sample. Select and validate on observables that can see the object, the dwell-time and transition structure along the axis of global activation, and discriminate among mechanistic rivals on those observables by model comparison or simulation-based inference, which the group already has \parencite{hashemi2024}.

Stated that way, the paper is the how-possibly half of a well-formed project, and its real claim is the testable one it has not yet tested. I do not think that is a small thing to have done. I do think it is a different thing from what the title says.

\section{Limits of this critique}
What is said here does not touch the fixed-point and escape-time analysis, which stands on its own; nor the cross-modal result inherited from the earlier paper, that cascades in EEG precede co-activation events in BOLD, which survived the authors' own surrogate test \parencite{rabuffo2021} and which no analysis of BOLD alone can address; nor the authors' statement that they were not fitting. It touches the step from the model to the brain, and only that step. One request falls out of it that costs the authors nothing: the tolerance. The model's output is a law and the data are one sample path, so ``within tolerance'' needs the sampling variability of each statistic across sessions or subjects. Without that number the set of admitted models has no boundary, and neither this critique nor the paper's claim can be made quantitative. \NUM{the numerical gates are model-internal, on a public 76-region connectome rather than the authors' subject, so they bear on the reimplementation and on the statistics, not on the paper's runs}

